      "brief": "You are the film's editor, working in the sandbox computer. Your inputs give you the checked page as a file in /inputs (film.html), the sheet as text and the tester's report (it gives the milliseconds a frame takes to render). The edit node after you renders the film from the timeline you write, with ffmpeg, from the page itself: every piece of the picture is a card (a complete copy of the page, rendered frame by frame by calling its window.renderAt), and the film's sound is the page's own: the edit node opens one more copy of the page, with no data-offset, and records what its window.renderAudio makes for the whole film.\nWrite cut.py with workspace_write and run it with python3. It reads /inputs/*film.html, writes the sheet you were given to sheet.json first (from your input, exactly), and writes timeline.json:\n- {\"schema\": \"awwo.timeline/1\", \"name\": \"run-token\", \"output\": {\"width\": <sheet width>, \"height\": <sheet height>, \"fps\": <sheet fps>, \"crf\": 18, \"loudnessLUFS\": <sheet loudnessLUFS>, \"truePeakDb\": -1, \"stillsEverySeconds\": 4}, \"tracks\": { ... }}.\n- tracks.video: the film as pieces in order, each {\"id\": \"p1\", \"card\": <the page with data-offset=\"<the piece's start in seconds>\" added to its first <html tag>, \"seconds\": <the piece's length>}, with no transition and no \"at\". Plan with P = the tester's mean milliseconds per frame times 1.25 (its largest is a cold first frame; a card's frames take about the mean). If the whole film (its length x fps frames) would take more than 45 minutes at P, write output fps 24 instead of the sheet's (the page draws any moment, and the edit must end within an hour), and say so in the notes. Every piece but the last has the same length L: the longest whole number of beats of the sheet, at most 20 s, whose frames (L x fps) take at most 300 s at P (a card may take 360 s); L x fps must be a whole number of frames. The last piece is what is left. The pieces' seconds add up to the sheet's length exactly.\n- tracks.music: [{\"id\": \"score\", \"page\": <the page exactly as in /inputs, with no data-offset>, \"seconds\": <the sheet's length>, \"at\": 0}] (the page's own sound already starts and ends as the brief says, so no fades).\n- Checks in cut.py before it writes: every card's UTF-8 bytes at most 262,144; at most 40 cards and at most 120 s of cards; the whole timeline.json at most 1,900,000 bytes; the pieces' frames add up to length x fps. If one fails, print it and stop.\nPublish timeline.json into the field \"result\" (workspace_publish, path \"timeline.json\"), and notes.md into the field \"report\": the pieces (start, length, frames), the sound item, the timeline's bytes, and the milliseconds per frame you planned with. End with exactly {\"result\": \"\", \"report\": \"\"}. Keep your thinking short; this run ends after 25 minutes and about 20 model calls.\n\nAcceptance:\n- timeline.json passes cut.py's checks\n- The pieces add up to the sheet's length in whole frames\n\nThis is an official reference workflow awaiting execution. Use actual inputs; never present a demo, plan or static check as successful execution. List unverified items when execution or capabilities are unavailable. Do not deploy, send messages or connect production data.\nWrite everything in English: code, comments, words on screen, reports. No Chinese characters anywhere.",
