  "note": "Run on the live AwwO, in the operator’s own account and workspace, on 2026-10-26, every agent on Claude Opus 5.5 working in its own sandbox computer. The film answers an Opus 5.5 video prompt by Gorden Sun (x.com/Gorden_Sun/status/2105302007896797351, prompt 11755 of YouMind’s Opus 5.5 collection): a 3D sphere that bounces down a structure in time with music, lighting each step it lands on, the scene changing with each of three classical pieces. In AwwO’s version the sphere is a run token and the steps are canvas nodes. The canvas’s text was written for this film by the operator beforehand: the Director’s task (the prompt adapted to AwwO, with the facts the film may state and the look), the kernel contract every module writes to, each builder’s duty, and the tester’s and the editor’s tasks. What is seen and heard is what the canvas’s agents wrote; the three pieces (Grieg’s In the Hall of the Mountain King, the finale of Rossini’s William Tell overture and Offenbach’s Infernal Galop) are played by the page’s own code, not recordings. The canvas ran three times, each run after the first running only the nodes it named, on the kept outputs of the rest. The first (10:15 to 11:12 UTC) finished everything but the edit. The tester found that the page’s self-test could not finish in its browser, which draws three.js without a GPU at 1.5 to 3.4 s a frame, and made the self-test draw its samples at a quarter of the resolution, changing no test; after that the page passed 13 of its 13 tests and caught all 4 planted failures. The editor then wrote no timeline: it planned each frame at the tester’s slowest measured time and a half, against 180 s a piece, which came to 48 pieces of 1 s (an edit takes at most 40) and a timeline of 3.65 MB (at most 1.9 MB), so its own checks stopped it, and the edit refused what it was handed. In the second run (12:05 to 12:07) the editor ran again with a task that plans at the tester’s mean time and a quarter, 300 s a piece, and 24 frames a second where the whole film would take more than 45 minutes at 30: 12 pieces of 4 s at 24 fps. The third (13:19 to 13:45) ran the edit, which rendered each piece from the page and recorded the page’s own sound (window.renderAudio), with no model. The canvas’s Review node ran beside the edit and is not part of this case.",
  "verification": "Before publishing, the operator looked at the delivered film (1280×720, 24 fps, 48 s, −16 LUFS) one frame every two seconds and at frames in full, measured the picture’s change at each of the eleven joins between the pieces, and looked at its sound as a spectrogram. The token drops through a cave, along a track and onto a stage, lighting each node it lands on, amber, then green, then blue, with each piece’s title and composer as its scene opens and the bubbles “Me next!”, “Wait, I’m not done!”, “Retrying… 1 of 2”, “Who wired this?” and “Approved.” on the way; it ends on a Review node with “Approved.” and “AwwO”. Each lit node blooms into a bright glow, in the cave most of all. Every join falls on a beat and changes the picture as much as a landing on any other beat does (about 40 on a 0-255 scale); the largest changes are the two scene changes, at 16 and 32 s. The sound plays the three pieces’ tunes on the beat."
